Here is a C++ program that calculates the nth Fibonacci number using recursion.

### Important Note on Efficiency
While the recursive solution is elegant and easy to understand, it has a time complexity of $O(2^n)$. This means it becomes extremely slow for even moderately large values of $n$ (e.g., $n > 40$) because it recalculates the same values many times.

For production code or large $n$, an iterative approach or memoization is preferred. However, below is the direct recursive implementation as requested.

```cpp
#include <iostream>

// Function to calculate the nth Fibonacci number recursively
// Base cases: F(0) = 0, F(1) = 1
long long fibonacci(int n) {
    if (n < 0) {
        throw std::invalid_argument("Fibonacci is not defined for negative numbers.");
    }
    if (n == 0) {
        return 0;
    }
    if (n == 1) {
        return 1;
    }
    // Recursive step: F(n) = F(n-1) + F(n-2)
    return fibonacci(n - 1) + fibonacci(n - 2);
}

int main() {
    int n;

    std::cout << "Enter a non-negative integer n: ";

    // Check if input is valid
    if (!(std::cin >> n) || n < 0) {
        std::cerr << "Invalid input. Please enter a non-negative integer." << std::endl;
        return 1;
    }

    try {
        long long result = fibonacci(n);
        std::cout << "The " << n << "th Fibonacci number is: " << result << std::endl;
    } catch (const std::exception& e) {
        std::cerr << "Error: " << e.what() << std::endl;
        return 1;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

